WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:03.891
Last time

2
00:00:03.990 --> 00:00:05.779
we found that a priority queue only

3
00:00:05.830 --> 00:00:07.230
has to promise one thing

4
00:00:07.230 --> 00:00:10.220
the smallest entry comes out first.

5
00:00:10.570 --> 00:00:13.400
A sorted list gives us much more than that

6
00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:16.180
and we paid for it with slow inserts.

7
00:00:16.180 --> 00:00:18.680
Can we promise only what we need

8
00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:21.810
and get fast inserts and fast removals?

9
00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:23.190
Yes.

10
00:00:23.190 --> 00:00:24.930
The structure is called a heap

11
00:00:24.930 --> 00:00:27.280
and it fits in an ordinary array.

12
00:00:27.860 --> 00:00:29.674
In this video we'll see what

13
00:00:29.710 --> 00:00:32.228
a heap looks like, how it fits in an array

14
00:00:32.290 --> 00:00:34.666
and how insert and remove min repair

15
00:00:34.730 --> 00:00:37.420
it in about log n steps.

16
00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:39.950
A heap has two rules.

17
00:00:39.950 --> 00:00:41.750
The first is about shape.

18
00:00:41.750 --> 00:00:44.510
It is a complete binary tree

19
00:00:44.510 --> 00:00:47.390
every level is full, except maybe the last

20
00:00:47.390 --> 00:00:49.040
which is filled from the left.

21
00:00:49.390 --> 00:00:51.364
The second is about order.

22
00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:53.504
Every entry is smaller than

23
00:00:53.580 --> 00:00:55.620
or equal to its children.

24
00:00:55.620 --> 00:00:58.870
So the smallest entry is always at the root.

25
00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:00.485
That is all.

26
00:01:00.590 --> 00:01:02.555
Brothers and sisters are not ordered

27
00:01:02.610 --> 00:01:04.095
with respect to each other.

28
00:01:04.150 --> 00:01:07.134
A node on one branch can be larger than

29
00:01:07.210 --> 00:01:09.700
a node deeper on another branch.

30
00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:12.410
Because the tree is complete

31
00:01:12.410 --> 00:01:14.250
we do not need pointers.

32
00:01:14.250 --> 00:01:17.130
We can store it in an array, level by level

33
00:01:17.130 --> 00:01:18.440
from left to right.

34
00:01:18.790 --> 00:01:21.120
The root is at index zero.

35
00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:23.760
Its children are at one and two.

36
00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:26.900
Their children are at three, four, five

37
00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:27.630
six.

38
00:01:27.980 --> 00:01:30.571
The rule: the children of index j

39
00:01:30.650 --> 00:01:34.250
are at two j plus one, and two j plus two.

40
00:01:34.250 --> 00:01:37.410
And the parent of index j is j minus one

41
00:01:37.410 --> 00:01:39.620
divided by two, rounded down.

42
00:01:39.970 --> 00:01:40.900
Careful

43
00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:43.520
this assumes the array starts at zero.

44
00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:45.405
Some books start at one

45
00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:47.355
and then the formulas are two

46
00:01:47.420 --> 00:01:49.400
i and two i plus one.

47
00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:51.130
Check which one you are reading.

48
00:01:51.710 --> 00:01:53.825
How tall is a complete tree?

49
00:01:53.900 --> 00:01:55.855
Each level holds twice as many

50
00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:57.223
nodes as the one above

51
00:01:57.280 --> 00:02:00.083
so n entries fit in about log

52
00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:02.190
base two of n levels.

53
00:02:02.540 --> 00:02:05.290
A thousand entries: ten levels.

54
00:02:05.290 --> 00:02:09.260
A million entries: twenty levels.

55
00:02:09.610 --> 00:02:11.781
Everything we do will follow one path

56
00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:13.460
from the top to the bottom

57
00:02:13.460 --> 00:02:15.440
or from the bottom to the top.

58
00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:18.650
So it costs about the number of levels.

59
00:02:19.230 --> 00:02:20.500
Insert.

60
00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:21.140
First

61
00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:23.280
put the new entry at the end of the array.

62
00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:25.020
That keeps the tree complete

63
00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:28.060
but the order rule may now be broken

64
00:02:28.060 --> 00:02:30.350
between the new entry and its parent.

65
00:02:30.700 --> 00:02:33.750
So we compare the new entry with its parent.

66
00:02:33.750 --> 00:02:36.010
If it is smaller, we swap them

67
00:02:36.010 --> 00:02:37.570
and repeat one level up.

68
00:02:37.570 --> 00:02:40.500
This climb is called up-heap.

69
00:02:40.850 --> 00:02:43.860
Take a heap with nine, fifteen and twenty

70
00:02:43.860 --> 00:02:45.320
and insert four.

71
00:02:45.320 --> 00:02:48.980
Four goes in at index three, under fifteen.

72
00:02:48.980 --> 00:02:50.860
It is smaller: swap.

73
00:02:50.860 --> 00:02:52.660
Now it is under nine.

74
00:02:52.660 --> 00:02:54.900
Smaller again: swap.

75
00:02:54.900 --> 00:02:56.040
It is the root.

76
00:02:56.040 --> 00:02:58.450
Two comparisons, two swaps.

77
00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:00.570
Now insert twelve.

78
00:03:00.570 --> 00:03:02.090
It goes under nine

79
00:03:02.090 --> 00:03:04.430
and twelve is not smaller than nine

80
00:03:04.430 --> 00:03:05.870
so we stop at once.

81
00:03:05.870 --> 00:03:07.280
One comparison.

82
00:03:07.860 --> 00:03:09.610
Remove min.

83
00:03:09.610 --> 00:03:11.730
The smallest entry is at the root

84
00:03:11.730 --> 00:03:12.790
so we take it.

85
00:03:12.790 --> 00:03:15.050
But now the root is empty

86
00:03:15.050 --> 00:03:17.020
and the tree has a hole.

87
00:03:17.370 --> 00:03:18.807
Fill the hole with the last

88
00:03:18.860 --> 00:03:20.060
entry of the array.

89
00:03:20.060 --> 00:03:21.935
The shape is complete again

90
00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:23.579
but this entry at the root

91
00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:25.610
is probably too large.

92
00:03:25.960 --> 00:03:27.610
So we sink it.

93
00:03:27.610 --> 00:03:30.010
Compare it with its two children

94
00:03:30.010 --> 00:03:31.670
pick the smaller child

95
00:03:31.670 --> 00:03:35.070
and if that child is smaller than our entry

96
00:03:35.070 --> 00:03:36.030
swap.

97
00:03:36.030 --> 00:03:38.070
Repeat one level down.

98
00:03:38.070 --> 00:03:40.280
This is down-heap.

99
00:03:40.630 --> 00:03:42.189
Why the smaller child?

100
00:03:42.260 --> 00:03:44.376
Because it moves up to become the

101
00:03:44.440 --> 00:03:46.077
parent of the other child

102
00:03:46.140 --> 00:03:47.775
and it must not be larger

103
00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:49.550
than its new child.

104
00:03:49.900 --> 00:03:50.930
An example.

105
00:03:50.930 --> 00:03:53.490
Remove two from this heap.

106
00:03:53.490 --> 00:03:55.990
Seven, the last entry, becomes the root.

107
00:03:55.990 --> 00:03:58.870
Its children are nine and four.

108
00:03:58.870 --> 00:04:01.130
Four is smaller, and smaller than seven

109
00:04:01.130 --> 00:04:02.410
so swap.

110
00:04:02.410 --> 00:04:05.570
Now seven has only a child twenty

111
00:04:05.570 --> 00:04:06.790
which is larger.

112
00:04:06.790 --> 00:04:07.700
Stop.

113
00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:09.930
Pause and predict.

114
00:04:09.930 --> 00:04:12.930
Here is the heap we just made, as an array

115
00:04:12.930 --> 00:04:19.590
two, nine, four, fifteen, twelve, twenty

116
00:04:19.590 --> 00:04:20.120
seven.

117
00:04:20.470 --> 00:04:22.160
Is this array sorted?

118
00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:23.785
Is the second smallest entry

119
00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:25.710
always at index one?

120
00:04:26.060 --> 00:04:27.110
No.

121
00:04:27.110 --> 00:04:28.770
The second smallest is four

122
00:04:28.770 --> 00:04:32.030
and it sits at index two, after nine.

123
00:04:32.030 --> 00:04:34.256
A heap is not a sorted array.

124
00:04:34.330 --> 00:04:35.998
It is only organized well enough

125
00:04:36.050 --> 00:04:37.660
to find the smallest.

126
00:04:38.010 --> 00:04:39.981
That is why a heap can be

127
00:04:40.060 --> 00:04:41.340
repaired so cheaply.

128
00:04:41.340 --> 00:04:42.970
It has a lot of freedom.

129
00:04:43.550 --> 00:04:46.719
Each step of up-heap or down-heap looks

130
00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:48.880
at a constant number of entries.

131
00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:51.520
Up-heap does one comparison per level.

132
00:04:51.520 --> 00:04:53.670
Down-heap does up to two.

133
00:04:54.020 --> 00:04:56.501
And there are at most as many levels

134
00:04:56.570 --> 00:04:58.370
as the height of the tree.

135
00:04:58.370 --> 00:05:01.780
So both operations cost about log n.

136
00:05:02.130 --> 00:05:04.577
The worst case for insert is when every

137
00:05:04.640 --> 00:05:06.720
new entry is the smallest so far

138
00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:09.320
so it climbs all the way up.

139
00:05:09.320 --> 00:05:10.480
A thousand entries

140
00:05:10.480 --> 00:05:12.454
inserted in decreasing order

141
00:05:12.520 --> 00:05:14.421
cost seven thousand nine hundred

142
00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:16.020
eighty-seven comparisons.

143
00:05:16.020 --> 00:05:18.280
That is about eight per entry

144
00:05:18.280 --> 00:05:19.570
close to the height.

145
00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:22.651
Sixty-four thousand entries cost eight hundred

146
00:05:22.710 --> 00:05:24.590
ninety-four thousand comparisons

147
00:05:24.590 --> 00:05:26.410
about fourteen per entry.

148
00:05:26.410 --> 00:05:29.410
The cost per entry grows like log n

149
00:05:29.410 --> 00:05:30.640
not like n.

150
00:05:31.220 --> 00:05:34.110
Why is the repair correct?

151
00:05:34.110 --> 00:05:35.610
During up-heap

152
00:05:35.610 --> 00:05:37.784
everything obeys the order rule

153
00:05:37.850 --> 00:05:39.949
except possibly the moving

154
00:05:40.030 --> 00:05:41.780
entry and its parent.

155
00:05:42.130 --> 00:05:45.528
Each swap pushes that single possible

156
00:05:45.620 --> 00:05:47.540
violation one level up

157
00:05:47.540 --> 00:05:49.840
and we stop when it is gone

158
00:05:49.840 --> 00:05:51.810
or when we reach the root.

159
00:05:52.160 --> 00:05:55.045
During down-heap, everything obeys the rule

160
00:05:55.110 --> 00:05:56.805
except possibly the moving

161
00:05:56.870 --> 00:05:58.250
entry and its children.

162
00:05:58.310 --> 00:05:59.812
Choosing the smaller child

163
00:05:59.870 --> 00:06:01.600
makes each swap safe.

164
00:06:01.950 --> 00:06:04.340
At most one place is ever broken

165
00:06:04.340 --> 00:06:06.120
and we always know where.

166
00:06:06.120 --> 00:06:08.110
That is the invariant.

167
00:06:08.690 --> 00:06:09.717
To sum up.

168
00:06:09.820 --> 00:06:11.594
A heap is a complete binary

169
00:06:11.660 --> 00:06:13.060
tree in an array

170
00:06:13.060 --> 00:06:16.250
where no child is smaller than its parent.

171
00:06:16.600 --> 00:06:19.738
Insert adds at the end and climbs.

172
00:06:19.830 --> 00:06:22.271
Remove min moves the last entry

173
00:06:22.350 --> 00:06:24.470
to the root and sinks it.

174
00:06:24.470 --> 00:06:26.800
Both cost about log n.

175
00:06:27.150 --> 00:06:29.420
Next, the code in Python and Java.

176
00:06:29.420 --> 00:06:30.300
After that

177
00:06:30.300 --> 00:06:32.420
what if you already have all the entries

178
00:06:32.420 --> 00:06:34.350
and want a heap from them right away?

179
00:06:34.977 --> 00:06:36.551
Let's write the heap in Java

180
00:06:36.607 --> 00:06:38.097
and watch it work.

181
00:06:38.677 --> 00:06:40.895
Three small static methods turn

182
00:06:40.967 --> 00:06:42.487
the formulas into code.

183
00:06:42.487 --> 00:06:45.267
Left is two j plus one.

184
00:06:45.267 --> 00:06:47.747
Right is two j plus two.

185
00:06:47.747 --> 00:06:51.157
Parent is j minus one, divided by two.

186
00:06:51.507 --> 00:06:52.717
In Java

187
00:06:52.717 --> 00:06:55.507
integer division truncates toward zero

188
00:06:55.577 --> 00:06:59.015
so j minus one over two is already rounded

189
00:06:59.097 --> 00:07:01.337
down for every j above zero.

190
00:07:01.337 --> 00:07:04.497
For j equal to zero it gives zero

191
00:07:04.497 --> 00:07:06.317
the root is its own parent.

192
00:07:06.317 --> 00:07:07.850
That is harmless here

193
00:07:07.917 --> 00:07:09.852
but it is a different surprise

194
00:07:09.917 --> 00:07:11.787
from Python's minus one.

195
00:07:12.137 --> 00:07:13.574
Our loops never ask for the

196
00:07:13.627 --> 00:07:15.097
parent of the root.

197
00:07:15.677 --> 00:07:17.442
Insert adds the item to the

198
00:07:17.507 --> 00:07:18.707
end of the array list

199
00:07:18.707 --> 00:07:21.117
then calls up-heap on the new index.

200
00:07:21.467 --> 00:07:24.557
Up-heap loops while j is above zero.

201
00:07:24.557 --> 00:07:27.597
A helper called less compares two positions

202
00:07:27.597 --> 00:07:28.877
using the comparator

203
00:07:28.877 --> 00:07:30.458
and counts the comparison.

204
00:07:30.517 --> 00:07:32.276
If the entry is not less than

205
00:07:32.337 --> 00:07:33.917
its parent we stop

206
00:07:33.917 --> 00:07:36.547
otherwise we swap and move up.

207
00:07:36.897 --> 00:07:39.428
The fields comparisons and swaps

208
00:07:39.507 --> 00:07:41.127
let us measure the work.

209
00:07:41.127 --> 00:07:43.107
The heap holds any type

210
00:07:43.107 --> 00:07:46.377
and the comparator decides what small means.

211
00:07:46.957 --> 00:07:52.227
Here is a real run, inserting fifteen, nine

212
00:07:52.227 --> 00:07:57.637
twenty, four, twelve, seven and two.

213
00:07:57.987 --> 00:08:00.311
Inserting four took two comparisons

214
00:08:00.377 --> 00:08:03.577
and two swaps: it climbed to the root.

215
00:08:03.577 --> 00:08:05.777
Inserting twelve took one comparison

216
00:08:05.777 --> 00:08:07.037
and no swaps.

217
00:08:07.037 --> 00:08:08.587
It stopped at once.

218
00:08:09.167 --> 00:08:11.397
Remove min saves the root

219
00:08:11.397 --> 00:08:13.617
removes the last item from the list

220
00:08:13.617 --> 00:08:15.673
and if the heap is not empty

221
00:08:15.737 --> 00:08:17.470
puts that item at the root

222
00:08:17.537 --> 00:08:19.087
and calls down-heap.

223
00:08:19.437 --> 00:08:22.207
Down-heap loops while there is a left child.

224
00:08:22.207 --> 00:08:23.904
It picks the smaller child

225
00:08:23.967 --> 00:08:25.471
using the right one only if

226
00:08:25.527 --> 00:08:27.077
it exists and is less.

227
00:08:27.147 --> 00:08:29.051
It stops if that child is

228
00:08:29.127 --> 00:08:30.287
not less than the entry

229
00:08:30.287 --> 00:08:32.557
otherwise it swaps and moves down.

230
00:08:32.907 --> 00:08:34.297
Note the short circuit

231
00:08:34.357 --> 00:08:36.041
the check that the right child

232
00:08:36.097 --> 00:08:39.256
exists comes first, with a logical and

233
00:08:39.337 --> 00:08:40.796
so Java never reads past

234
00:08:40.857 --> 00:08:42.147
the end of the list.

235
00:08:42.727 --> 00:08:45.977
Removing from the heap: two comes out first

236
00:08:45.977 --> 00:08:50.257
and the rest follow in order: two, four

237
00:08:50.257 --> 00:08:55.674
seven, nine, twelve, fifteen, twenty.

238
00:08:55.817 --> 00:08:57.932
Each removal cost at most

239
00:08:58.017 --> 00:08:59.647
three comparisons here.

240
00:08:59.997 --> 00:09:01.689
Taking everything out gave us

241
00:09:01.747 --> 00:09:03.567
the entries in sorted order.

242
00:09:03.567 --> 00:09:05.597
That will become heap sort.

243
00:09:06.177 --> 00:09:08.347
Does our heap match the library's?

244
00:09:08.347 --> 00:09:11.057
We offered the same seven numbers to both.

245
00:09:11.407 --> 00:09:13.167
The arrays are identical.

246
00:09:13.237 --> 00:09:15.473
Java's Priority Queue uses the same

247
00:09:15.537 --> 00:09:17.253
layout and the same repair

248
00:09:17.317 --> 00:09:19.141
so what we write here is

249
00:09:19.217 --> 00:09:20.607
what runs inside it.

250
00:09:21.187 --> 00:09:22.937
Three common mistakes.

251
00:09:22.937 --> 00:09:23.817
One

252
00:09:23.817 --> 00:09:27.427
one-based formulas with a zero-based list.

253
00:09:27.777 --> 00:09:30.820
Two: reading the right child before

254
00:09:30.907 --> 00:09:32.737
checking that it exists.

255
00:09:33.087 --> 00:09:33.798
Three

256
00:09:33.917 --> 00:09:36.617
comparing objects with the double equals sign

257
00:09:36.677 --> 00:09:39.272
or with a subtraction in the comparator.

258
00:09:39.337 --> 00:09:41.643
A comparator that subtracts can

259
00:09:41.717 --> 00:09:43.757
overflow for large integers

260
00:09:43.757 --> 00:09:46.257
so use the compare method of Integer

261
00:09:46.257 --> 00:09:47.967
or compare to.

262
00:09:48.547 --> 00:09:51.737
So in Java: index arithmetic

263
00:09:51.737 --> 00:09:53.497
up-heap after an insert

264
00:09:53.497 --> 00:09:55.078
down-heap after a removal

265
00:09:55.137 --> 00:09:57.283
and a comparator that decides

266
00:09:57.357 --> 00:09:59.147
what small means.

267
00:09:59.497 --> 00:10:02.581
Next: building a heap from n entries

268
00:10:02.667 --> 00:10:05.497
in just about n steps.

